To implement `to_base(n, alphabet)`, I will first handle the sign: if `n` is negative, record the sign and work with the absolute value. Zero must map to the first character of the alphabet (index 0). For positive `n`, I'll repeatedly divide by the base (len(alphabet)) and collect remainders to build the digit string in reverse order, then reverse it. For `from_base(s, alphabet)`, I'll first check for empty string and raise ValueError immediately. Then handle the optional leading `-` sign, validate all characters are in the alphabet (raising ValueError if not), and convert by accumulating `value = value * base + digit_index`. Edge cases include zero, negative numbers, and ensuring very large integers (Python handles big ints natively) round-trip correctly.

