The approach uses a min-heap to efficiently select the smallest available element across all input iterables. Each heap entry is a tuple of (value, source_index, iterator), ensuring stability by using the source index as a tiebreaker when values are equal—this guarantees that elements from earlier sources appear first among equal values. We initialize the heap with the first element from each non-empty iterable, then repeatedly pop the smallest item, yield it, and push the next item from the same source (if any). Edge cases include empty iterables (ignored), infinite iterables (handled lazily), and iterables with duplicate values across sources (preserved in source order). The implementation avoids buffering by only storing one pending item per iterable at any time.

ANSWER:
