To implement `chunkAsync(source, size)` as a lazy async generator, I will iterate over the source using `for await` to support both sync and async iterables. For each chunk, I'll collect up to `size` items by pulling from the source one at a time. Crucially, I'll wrap the chunking logic in a try/finally block to ensure that if the consumer stops early (e.g., via `break` or `return`), I call `source.return()` to signal early termination and allow cleanup (e.g., closing streams). Edge cases handled: empty source yields nothing; non-integer or zero/negative `size` will throw (as per typical JS conventions); infinite sources work because we only pull as needed.

```javascript
async function* chunkAsync(source, size) {
  if (typeof size !== 'number' || !Number.isInteger(size) || size <= 0) {
    throw new RangeError('size must be a positive integer');
  }

  const iterator = source[Symbol.asyncIterator] ? source[Symbol.asyncIterator]() : source[Symbol.iterator]();

  try {
    while (true) {
      const chunk = [];
      for (let i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        const result = await iterator.next();
        if (result.done) {
          if (chunk.length > 0) {
            yield chunk;
          }
          return;
        }
        chunk.push(result.value);
      }
      yield chunk;
    }
  } finally {
    if (typeof iterator.return === 'function') {
      await iterator.return();
    }
  }
}

module.exports = chunkAsync;
```

