hjs-012.text:
To implement `zipLongest(fill, ...iterables)` lazily, I will create a generator function that maintains an array of iterator objects derived from the input iterables. On each iteration, it pulls one value from each live (not yet exhausted) iterator: if an iterator is still active, it yields its value; otherwise, it yields the `fill` value. Once all iterators are exhausted, the generator stops. Edge cases include: no iterables (returns empty iterator), all iterables exhausted immediately, and ensuring iterators are only advanced once per yielded row to preserve laziness.

ANSWER:
```javascript
function* zipLongest(fill, ...iterables) {
  const iterators = iterables.map(iterable => iterable[Symbol.iterator]());
  let doneCount = 0;
  const totalIterators = iterators.length;

  while (doneCount < totalIterators) {
    const row = [];
    let currentDoneCount = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < iterators.length; i++) {
      const iterator = iterators[i];
      if (!iterator) {
        row.push(fill);
        currentDoneCount++;
        continue;
      }

      const result = iterator.next();
      if (result.done) {
        row.push(fill);
        iterators[i] = null;
        currentDoneCount++;
      } else {
        row.push(result.value);
      }
    }

    doneCount = currentDoneCount;
    if (doneCount < totalIterators) {
      yield row;
    }
  }
}

module.exports = zipLongest;
```

