To implement `Split<S, D>`, I’ll use recursive conditional types with template literal inference to peel off segments of the string separated by the delimiter `D`. For non-empty `D`, I’ll match up to the first occurrence of `D` and recurse on the remainder; for empty `D`, I’ll split into characters by matching one character at a time. Edge cases: splitting an empty string must yield `['']`, and joining an empty tuple must yield `''`. For `Trim<S>`, I’ll recursively strip leading and trailing spaces using conditional types, handling the case where trimming results in an empty string (which should become `''`, not `[]`). I’ll ensure all operations preserve literal types and avoid inference issues by using distributive conditionals (`infer` with `extends string`) and explicit base cases.

```typescript
export type Split<S, D extends string> = 
  D extends '' ? 
    S extends `${infer Head}${infer Tail}` ? 
      [Head, ...Split<Tail, D>] : ['']
    : S extends '' ? [''] :
      SplitString<S, D>;

type SplitString<S, D extends string> = 
  S extends `${infer Prefix}${D}${infer Suffix}` ? 
    [Prefix, ...Split<Suffix, D>] : 
  [S];

export type Join<T extends any[], D extends string> = 
  T extends [] ? '' :
  T extends [infer Head] ? `${Head}` :
  T extends [infer Head, ...infer Tail] ? 
    `${Head}${D}${Join<Tail, D>}` :
  string;

export type Trim<S extends string> = 
  S extends ` ${infer Rest}` ? Trim<Rest> :
  S extends `${infer Rest} ` ? Trim<Rest> :
  S;
```

